### 31.3

Each Party shall update notice details promptly. Retain delivery or acknowledgement evidence and resend failed notices where reasonably practicable. Sending alone is not conclusive proof of receipt or statutory service. Applicable Law prevails.

## 32. FORCE MAJEURE

### 32.1

A Party is not liable for delay to the extent caused by an event beyond its reasonable control that could not reasonably have been prevented or overcome, including natural disaster, fire, flood, epidemic, war, civil disorder, government restriction, widespread telecommunications failure, or major transport disruption.

### 32.2

The affected Party shall notify the other promptly, provide reasonable evidence, mitigate the effect, and resume performance as soon as reasonably possible.

### 32.3

Relief applies only to obligations actually prevented and to the extent permitted by law. It does not excuse money already legally due, Customer refunds, misuse of data or funds, or preventable failures. Ordinary staffing shortages, lack of funds, stock shortages and foreseeable seasonal demand do not automatically qualify.

## 33. GOVERNING LAW AND DISPUTE RESOLUTION

### 33.1

This Agreement is governed by the laws of Nepal.

### 33.2

The Parties shall first attempt in good faith to resolve a dispute through their designated representatives within fifteen days after written notice.

### 33.3

Nothing prevents a Customer or Party from using a statutory complaint, mediation, regulatory, or consumer-protection mechanism.

